  "verdict": {
    "US": "alpha_not_significant_even_in_mkt_only",
    "TW": "alpha_not_significant_even_in_mkt_only",
    "note": "Variance-attribution (PC1) leaves alpha LEVEL unchanged by construction (zero-mean market-neutral factor raises R2/beta only). The alpha-absorption test is the premium-attribution stage (mean-bearing leave-out sector-long factor)."
  },
  "honest_conclusion": "US: a real semiconductor ETF (SMH) shows a headline alpha of 9.2%/yr vs SPY (t=1.91, marginal). On a clean disjoint leave-out proxy the sector alpha point estimate shrinks substantially (11.4%->4.0%/yr) once a disjoint sector-long factor is added, and becomes insignificant (t=0.85) -- but the mkt-only alpha was itself only marginally significant (t=1.88, p=0.060) and the result is split-sensitive (35-split residual-alpha range [-5.6%, 17.0%], 74% of splits insignificant). Evidence is SUGGESTIVE that the sector alpha is mostly unnamed common-factor exposure, not decisive. TW: no significant alpha to begin with (t=0.53), so there is nothing to absorb; TW sector cohesion is weaker (PC1 explains 46% vs US 63%). Variance-attribution (market-neutral PC1) raises R2/beta sharply but cannot move the alpha LEVEL by construction -- the comovement is a strong common factor, but the alpha-level question is answered only by the mean-bearing premium-attribution stage."
}